  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tonight still looks like the best candidate so \nfar for fairly widespread frost, especially west of NC 17 as \nskies remain clear and winds inland will likely be calm with \nhigh pressure building overhead tonight. As is typical in these \nenvironments, deterministic guidance for lows is not aggressive \nenough, so blended in NBM 10th percentile and the previous \nforecast to keep forecast lows into the mid to upper 30s. Could \nsee a few well sheltered areas make a run towards the low 30s \nbut this should be rather isolated. Frost Advisory continues\nfor areas along and west of Hwy 17 overnight. Tuesday night is \nforecast to remain slightly warmer as high cirrus is forecast to\nstream into ENC from the west preventing good radiational \ncooling Tue night limiting frost potential. So while it is \nunlikely tomorrow night it's not completely out of the question \nfor inland sheltered spots. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Not much has changed with this weekends system\nas there remains good agreement amongst guidance for \nincreasingly unsettled weather across the eastern CONUS. A \ncomplex mid-level low will deepen over the region and steer \nmultiple shortwaves over the Mid-Atlantic. While there are still\nsome differences in exact details that will need to be hashed\nout in the coming days it remains likely that a complex low will\nsweep across the Ohio River Valley and Mid-Atlantic Wed into\nThursday with a coastal low developing off the Mid-Atlantic\ncoast Thurs into Friday with this coastal low then deepening\noffshore as it meanders around the the Northeast this weekend \nthough its intensity and track remain uncertain. Probabilistic \nguidance favors a northern track for these areas of low \npressure, which would keep heavier precipitation away from our \narea though isolated to widely scattered showers wouldn't be out\nof the question. Either way not expecting much QPF from any \nshower that impacts the area Thurs into Fri before we dry out.\n\nThe biggest threat with this aforementioned low will be the\nassociated gusty winds. In general, probabilities for a period \nof 20-30 mph W-NW winds and gusts potentially in excess of \n35-45 mph Fri into Sat remain high. If this is realized, at \nleast some coastal flooding concerns could arise soundside along\nthe Outer Banks. Given spread of guidance, it is too early to \nstate the potential magnitude of impacts. Stay tuned for future \nupdates.\n\nBehind the system, unseasonably cool air will flood into the\narea knocking highs down into the 50s for the weekend and offer\nthe first real opportunity for a freeze Sat night and Sun night.",
